---
import lastAlive from "../data/sancho-last-alive.json";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { emph } from "../lib/text.js";
import ramon from "../data/ramon-lerena-rosario.json";
import mauricio from "../data/mauricio-casimira-montano.json";
import alejandros from "../data/the-three-alejandros.json";
import Spine from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Spine.astro";
import spines from "../data/descent-spines.json";

/* The two descents drawn the same way, so the eye can do the comparing. Both
   start in Spain, both cross, both spread across the Banda Oriental — and they
   never touch. Generation 1 of each is the Spanish couple named on a River
   Plate act; neither has been found in a Spanish register yet, so both are
   marked as the joint that cannot bear weight. */
const spine = (house) => spines
  .filter((r) => r.house === house)
  .map((r) => Object.fromEntries(
    Object.entries(r)
      .filter(([k, v]) => k !== "house" && v !== "" && v !== "-")
      .map(([k, v]) => [k, k === "weak" ? v === "yes" : v])));

const sanchoHouse = spine("sancho");
const ramonHouse = spine("ramon");
const argentineLine = spine("argentine");
const ramonRows = ramon.filter((r) => r.generation && r.grade && r.grade !== "grade");
const mauricioRows = mauricio.filter((r) => r.child && r.grade && r.grade !== "grade");
import dUruguayFourDepartments from "../data/uruguay-four-departments.json";
import d1812UnionAct from "../data/1812-union-act.json";
import fs20 from "../data/fs-session-2026-09-20.json";
import gl from "../data/gonzalez-lerena.json";
import colla from "../data/rosario-colla-household.json";
import h97 from "../data/household-97-serena.json";
import h229 from "../data/household-229-pedro-antonio.json";
import sweep from "../data/census-seven-spellings.json";
import dom from "../data/dominga-carballo-1836.json";
import carts from "../data/luis-llerena-carts.json";
import maria from "../data/maria-llerena-casa-2.json";
import filmmap from "../data/film-7499043-map.json";
import sanjose from "../data/alberto-lerena-larriera.json";
---
<Base title="Uruguay"
      eyebrow="Two Lerena families, two Spanish origins, and neither of them is his"
      dek="There are two documented Lerena families in nineteenth-century Uruguay, and on 17 September 2026 a baptism act of 1812 settled what they are to each other: nothing. One came from Córdoba in Andalusia, the other from Valdemoro outside Madrid. The surname entered the Banda Oriental twice, from opposite ends of Spain.">

<section class="blk">
  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0">
      <strong>Why this page exists.</strong> The surname is rare, so it was natural to assume the
      Uruguayan Lerenas were one kindred. <strong>They are two</strong>, and both are documented from
      parish and civil registers rather than from anybody&rsquo;s tree. Keeping them apart is not
      pedantry: it is the difference between a family tree and a wish.
      <a href={u("/argentina/")}>Argentina &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>The Spine &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The two houses</h2>
  <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1rem">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Sancho&rsquo;s house &mdash; Canelones, then Montevideo</h3>
      <p><strong>SANCHO LLERENA</strong> married <strong>María Fernández</strong> at Canelones on
        <strong>8 January 1791</strong>. The act calls him <em>natural de Córdova la Llana</em> and names
        his parents, <strong>Félix Llerena and Rosalía Aguilar</strong>. A dragoon; in 1798 he swore an
        oath and could not sign his name.</p>
      <p>Eight children between 1793 and 1815, and out of them the <strong>Montevideo trunk</strong> —
        the <em>Lerena Lenguas</em> and the <em>Lerena Traibel</em>. His son <strong>Avelino</strong>
        (1815&ndash;1890) married <strong>Ambrosia Traibel y Centeno</strong> and fathered the second of
        those branches.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Ramón&rsquo;s house &mdash; Rosario, then the interior</h3>
      <p><strong>RAMÓN SERENA</strong> — so the act writes him; the indexes say Lerena and Gerena —
        <em>natural de <strong>Valdemoro en Castilla la Nueva</strong></em>, married
        <strong>María Josefa González</strong>, <em>natural de la Ysla de Canarias, <strong>Lanzarote</strong></em>.
        He was fathering children from about 1800, and his sons were born in Uruguay: Alejandro at
        <strong>Rosario</strong>, Mauricio at <strong>Colla</strong> — the Rosario district of
        <strong>Colonia</strong>.</p>
      <p>His own parents are <strong>Salvador Serena and Ana de Cubas</strong>, which is where the current
        tree&rsquo;s otherwise unexplained &ldquo;Ramón Llerena <em>Cubas</em>&rdquo; comes from. From his
        sons come the <strong>Trinidad kindred</strong> of Flores and a spread across San José, Florida,
        Salto and Montevideo.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>They are not one family, and an act of 1812 says so in five words</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This was the question worth asking, because if the two houses were one the Uruguayan picture would
      close. <strong>They are not, and the answer did not come from arithmetic in the end. It came from a
      sentence.</strong></p>
    <p>On <strong>15 November 1812</strong>, at the <strong>Iglesia Matriz of Montevideo</strong>, Don Juan
      Lloberas baptised <strong>Manuel Lorenzo</strong>, born the day before, and wrote down where the
      parents came from:</p>
    <blockquote style="margin:1rem 0;padding-left:1rem;border-left:3px solid var(--rule,#d8d2c6)">
      <p style="margin:0"><em>&hellip;hijo legítimo de <strong>RAMON SERENA natural de VALDEMORO en
        CASTILLA LA NUEVA</strong>, y de <strong>María Josefa González natural de la Ysla de Canarias,
        LANZAROTE</strong>. Abuelos paternos <strong>Salvador Serena y Ana de Cubas</strong>; id. maternos
        <strong>Marcial de León González è Ysavel Lorenza</strong>.</em></p>
    </blockquote>
    <p><strong>Valdemoro is twenty-seven kilometres south of Madrid. Sancho was <em>natural de Córdova la
      Llana</em>, in Andalusia, five hundred kilometres away.</strong> Two separate Spanish emigrations,
      two separate arrivals, one rare surname. <a href={u("/searched/")}>Read the act &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>The arithmetic had already said they could not be father and son — Alejandro married in 1825 so
      Ramón was born about 1775&ndash;85, while Sancho married in 1791 and had his first child in 1793 —
      and their fathers were different men, <strong>Félix</strong> and <strong>Salvador</strong>. The act
      closes what the arithmetic left open.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre,#8a7f5c);margin:1.4rem 0">
    <div class="lab">A coincidence, correctly distrusted, and now discharged</div>
    <p style="margin:.6rem 0 0">Before the act was read, this page recorded that Sancho&rsquo;s father is
      written <strong><em>Feliz GERENA</em></strong> on a Montevideo act of <strong>1811</strong> and
      Ramón&rsquo;s father <strong><em>Salvador GERENA</em></strong> on one of <strong>1812</strong> — and
      called it <em>&ldquo;a scribal habit of one place and one time rather than two families
      independently choosing the same variant&rdquo;</em>, a reason to look and not a reason to believe.</p>
    <p style="margin:.6rem 0 0"><strong>That was exactly right.</strong> The act itself writes
      <strong>SERENA</strong>, and both men were baptising children <strong>at the same font, one year
      apart</strong>. Two families of the same rare surname, in one small city, at one church — and from
      opposite ends of Spain. <strong>The resemblance was real and it meant nothing.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The two descents, drawn</h2>
  <p class="dek">The same four generations of each, side by side. <strong>Both begin with a Spanish
    couple named on a River Plate act and found in no Spanish register yet</strong> — so generation one
    of each is marked as the joint that cannot bear weight. Both cross the Atlantic, both spread across
    the Banda Oriental, <strong>and they never touch.</strong></p>
  <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.2rem;gap:26px">
    <div>
      <h3 style="margin:0 0 .4rem">Sancho&rsquo;s house &mdash; out of <em>Córdoba</em></h3>
      <p class="cap" style="margin:0 0 .6rem">Andalusia &rarr; Canelones 1791 &rarr; Montevideo</p>
      <Spine rows={sanchoHouse} u={u} archiveKey="lerena" idPrefix="sanchohouse-" />
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 style="margin:0 0 .4rem">Ramón&rsquo;s house &mdash; out of <em>Valdemoro</em></h3>
      <p class="cap" style="margin:0 0 .6rem">Castilla la Nueva &rarr; Rosario, Colonia &rarr; the interior</p>
      <Spine rows={ramonHouse} u={u} archiveKey="lerena" idPrefix="ramonhouse-" />
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:26px;gap:26px">
    <div>
      <h3 style="margin:0 0 .4rem">And the line this archive is looking for</h3>
      <p class="cap" style="margin:0 0 .6rem">Rosario de Santa Fe &rarr; the Cape &rarr; Brisbane</p>
      <Spine rows={argentineLine} u={u} archiveKey="lerena" idPrefix="argentineline-" />
    </div>
    <div style="align-self:center">
      <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--terra);margin:0">
        <div class="lab">Why it is drawn to the same scale</div>
        <p style="margin:.6rem 0 0">Both Uruguayan houses run <strong>four generations</strong> and reach
          back to a named couple in Spain. <strong>Pablo Armando&rsquo;s runs two, and stops at a pair of
          names on one card.</strong></p>
        <p style="margin:.6rem 0 0">That is not a gap in the research. It is the whole problem, and it is
          why the Uruguayan work is done <em>in parallel and in hope</em> rather than as his line.
          <strong>Nothing yet connects the left of this page to the right of it.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px"><strong>Five hundred kilometres apart in Spain, and about
    ninety in Uruguay.</strong> Sancho married at Canelones and Ramón baptised at the Iglesia Matriz of
    Montevideo — one year apart, in the same small city, at the same font — having come from opposite
    ends of the same country.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Ramón&rsquo;s house, record by record</h2>
  <p class="dek">Found on 17 September 2026 by searching the free FamilySearch collections
    <em>by parent</em> rather than by person. Every line is an index entry; where an image exists and has
    not been read, it says so.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:22%">Generation</th><th style="width:20%">Record</th><th>What it says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {ramonRows.map((r) => (
          <tr><td><span set:html={emph(r.generation)} /></td>
            <td><span set:html={emph(r.record)} /></td>
            <td><span set:html={emph(r["what it says"])} />
              <br /><span class="cap" set:html={emph(r.grade)} /></td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Mauricio and Casimira, and the children who spread across the interior</h2>
  <p class="dek">Mauricio Lerena married <strong>Casimira Montaño</strong> at San José de Mayo on
    3 February 1837. Their children are why the current family tree points at four different
    departments: <strong>they are not four leads, they are one family moving.</strong></p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:20%">Child</th><th style="width:24%">Record</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {mauricioRows.map((r) => (
          <tr><td><span set:html={emph(r.child)} /></td>
            <td><span set:html={emph(r.record)} /></td>
            <td><span set:html={emph(r.grade)} /></td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The Trinidad nest</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>At <strong>Trinidad</strong>, the small inland capital of Flores, six or seven Lerena men were
      fathering children between 1871 and 1895 — <strong>Bernabé, Vitalino, Ysaac, Cipriano, Fermín,
      Florencio</strong>. Two of them are now placed: <strong>Bernabé is Alejandro&rsquo;s son and
      Cipriano is Mauricio&rsquo;s, which makes them first cousins.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>The mothers are the other finding, and they are Basque</strong> —
      <em>Beloqui, Echeverry, Pelaez, Gazo, Barragán</em>. A Lerena kindred marrying Basque women in one
      small town speaks to the open question of where the surname comes from; it is evidence about the
      <strong>community they married into</strong>, not about the etymology, and it is recorded as that
      and nothing more.</p>
    <p><strong>And they married each other.</strong> Lerenas and Pelaezes at Trinidad intermarried across
      at least forty years, until by <strong>1885</strong> the church required a
      <strong>dispensation for consanguinity</strong> to let them do it again — twice in the third degree,
      once in the second and third accumulated. <strong>These are not two families who happened to meet.
      They are one intermarried kindred in a small inland town.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What would join the two houses, and what would join either of them to Pablo Armando</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>The houses will not be joined.</strong> That question is closed: Córdoba and Valdemoro
      are five hundred kilometres apart and the act is explicit. What is now open instead is
      <strong>Valdemoro</strong>, which is a finite and searchable place. <strong>Salvador Serena and Ana
      de Cubas were married in Spain</strong>, and that marriage is findable; so is Ramón&rsquo;s own
      baptism, about 1775&ndash;85. The archive has a hard Spanish place-name for this family where an
      hour ago it had none.</p>
    <p>And a second stream opens with it that nobody suspected: <strong>María Josefa González was a
      <em>canaria</em>, from Lanzarote</strong>. The Canary Islands fed their own migration into the River
      Plate, and no part of this archive has ever looked at it.</p>
    <p><strong>And now the thing this page must not be allowed to obscure.</strong>
      <strong>Neither house reaches Pablo Armando.</strong> There is no <em>Juan Carlos Lerena</em> in
      either family, in any generation, in any of the searches that built this page. He was born at
      <strong>Rosario de Santa Fe in Argentina</strong> in 1882, and the Argentine end says the same thing
      from three directions: the cathedral baptism index holds no Lerena for 1881&ndash;83, the 1895
      census holds no Lerena or Lerina household anywhere in Santa Fe province, and his own parents are
      named on exactly one document — <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>his enrolment card</a> — and nowhere
      else on earth.</p>
    <p><strong>So these two families are being built in parallel, and in hope.</strong> The hope is
      reasonable: the surname is rare, the River Plate is one world with a river through the middle of it,
      and <a href={u("/dna/")}>the DNA says the Argentine and Uruguayan hypotheses are genetically the same
      hypothesis</a>. <strong>The hope is not evidence, and nothing here is written as though it were.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-four">The four departments, and what depth means</h2>
  <div class="prose"><p>Coverage is not the same as discovery. What the four departments hold, and what searching them further would and would not buy.</p></div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr>{Object.keys(dUruguayFourDepartments[0] || {}).map((h) => <th>{h}</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {dUruguayFourDepartments.map((r) => (
          <tr>{Object.values(r).map((v, i) => (
            i === 0 ? <th set:html={emph(v)} /> : <td class="cap" set:html={emph(v)} />
          ))}</tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-1812">The act of 1812</h2>
  <div class="prose"><p>A union recorded in Canelones, and the godparents who come with it.</p></div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr>{Object.keys(d1812UnionAct[0] || {}).map((h) => <th>{h}</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {d1812UnionAct.map((r) => (
          <tr>{Object.values(r).map((v, i) => (
            i === 0 ? <th set:html={emph(v)} /> : <td class="cap" set:html={emph(v)} />
          ))}</tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-household">Sancho&rsquo;s household &mdash; eleven years at Canelones, and four people it owned</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>The count of Sancho&rsquo;s children has
      moved twice, and the file that held it was once called complete.</strong> An earlier list held
      <strong>four</strong> children under that name; the reconciled list <strong>supersedes</strong> it and
      names <strong>eight</strong>, born between 1793 and 1815. The padr&oacute;n of 1812 returns
      <strong>six</strong> in the household, which is not a contradiction: two were born after it, and
      Jos&eacute; Loreto, born 1806, is in no tree and no census and was found only because the surname was
      searched as <em>LORENA</em>. The likeliest reading of his absence from the padr&oacute;n is that he
      died before 1812 &mdash; but no burial has been looked for, and none is claimed.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>They lived at Canelones; they did not visit
      it.</strong> Jos&eacute; Loreto&rsquo;s baptism of 11 December 1806 calls Sancho and Mar&iacute;a
      <em>&ldquo;vecinos de esta Villa&rdquo;</em> &mdash; <strong>residents</strong>. That joins
      Agustina&rsquo;s baptism there in 1795, Dominga&rsquo;s in 1804, and Justa Pastora being called
      <em>natural de Canelones</em> in 1831. <strong>The family lived at Canelones for at least eleven
      years, and had moved to Montevideo, calle San Juan N&ordm; 60, by the padr&oacute;n of 1812.</strong>
      The godfather was fetched <em>from</em> Montevideo, which is the other direction.</p>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <strong>And the household explains a name.</strong>
      The act of 30 August 1795 at
      Canelones reads <em>&ldquo;bautize, puse el Santo Oleo y Crisma a <strong>AGUSTINA RAMONA, DE TRES
      DIAS NACIDA</strong>, hija legitima de D.<sup>n</sup> Sancho Lerena y de D.<sup>a</sup> Maria
      Fernandez, vecinos de la Villa&rdquo;</em> &mdash; which confirms the birth of 27 August 1795 that
      this register had carried from a tree and a census, and gives her a second forename. Her godfather
      was <strong>Don Fidel Rey, vecino de Montevideo</strong>. Four years later her <strong>younger brother</strong> was
      christened <strong>FIDEL</strong> &mdash; a forename that appears <strong>nowhere else</strong> in
      this family. <strong>This family names children for whoever stands at the font, five times over in the
      acts read here</strong>, so the likeliest source of the Lerena Fidel is that man. It is an
      inference from a pattern and not from a record.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> It also settles nothing about the <strong>other</strong>
      Fidel. A Montevideo death registration of 1889 names <em>&ldquo;Abuelos paternos FIDEL LERENA y
      MARIA RUEDAS&rdquo;</em>, and that plate was opened on 21 September. <strong>Whether that Fidel is
      Sancho&rsquo;s son remains unestablished, and nothing on the plate bears on it.</strong> The tree&rsquo;s
      death of 1822 &mdash; the one thing that would have kept them apart &mdash; is not in the record
      either. The two are still not merged: an unevidenced death is not evidence of survival.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p><strong>The padr&oacute;n counted four enslaved people in that house and named none of them.</strong></p>
    <p class="cap">Six weeks later, at the Montevideo Catedral on 26 September 1812, an act names one:
      <em>&ldquo;bautic&eacute; solemnemente a <strong>MARIA MERCEDES</strong>, que naci&oacute; antes de
      ayer, hija de <strong>JUANA</strong>, negra esclava de D.<sup>n</sup> Sancho Lerena y de D.<sup>a</sup>
      Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez: madrina Catalina Mart&iacute;nez.&rdquo;</em></p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>Juana</strong> &mdash; held by Sancho and Mar&iacute;a jointly, as the act says
      and the padr&oacute;n of the same month confirms. No age, no origin, no surname, no later record.
      Her daughter <strong>Mar&iacute;a Mercedes</strong>, born <strong>24 September 1812</strong> and
      baptised at two days old. Nothing is known of what became of her.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>That plate was opened for one reason.</strong> The row had rested on a machine
      transcript, and <strong>a machine is worst at proper nouns &mdash; while this woman&rsquo;s name is
      the only thing any record gives her.</strong> So it was the one that most needed a human to read it.
      It is <strong>JUANA</strong>, in the priest&rsquo;s own hand, film 007713375 image 02628, folio 136.
      This archive carries her name rather than a number.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>The same opening carries a trap this archive
      walked past.</strong> The act immediately before hers baptises <strong>another Mar&iacute;a
      Mercedes</strong>, on 25 September 1812 &mdash; <em>&ldquo;hija de <strong>TERESA ABELLEYRA</strong>,
      negra esclava de D.<sup>n</sup> <strong>RAMON ABELLEYRA</strong>&rdquo;</em>. Two enslaved girls of
      the same name, in two households, on consecutive days, on one page. <strong>And this register also
      holds a Teresa enslaved in Sancho&rsquo;s household, from the padr&oacute;n</strong> &mdash; a
      different woman, from a different document. The two must not be run together.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-fs-20sep">What FamilySearch answered on 20 September, and two controls worth more than the answers</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>One of these was found while running a <strong>control</strong> rather than while looking for it:
    the 1877 marriage of Gilberto Lerena and Julia Salvañach may move his parentage from
    <strong>tree</strong> to <strong>documented</strong>. It is not being claimed yet, and the reason is
    in the last row &mdash; <strong>one half of that same index entry is visibly wrong</strong>, which
    is the whole argument for opening the image before trusting the other half.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr>
        <th style="width:15rem">Finding</th><th style="width:16rem">What was searched</th>
        <th style="width:14rem">Result</th><th>Verdict</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {fs20.map((r) => { const c = Object.values(r); return (
          <tr>
            <th set:html={emph(c[0])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[1])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[2])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[3])} />
          </tr>); })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-sancho-alive">When was Sancho last alive? Fourteen records, and only four count</h2>
  <p class="dek">A man can be named in a document for sixty years after he dies. This archive holds
    fourteen records naming Sancho Llerena and asked a narrower question of each of them: does this one
    require him to have been <em>standing there</em>?</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>Four do, and the latest is 1812.</strong> He is
      godfather at the Iglesia Matriz in <strong>1789</strong>; a sworn witness at Canelones in
      <strong>1798</strong>, where he took an oath and could not sign it; a <em>padrino</em> at the
      Canelones confirmations in <strong>1804</strong>; and returned in the
      <strong>padr&oacute;n of 1812</strong> at calle San Juan No 60 &mdash; <em>Sancho, andaluz,
      45</em> &mdash; and <strong>a census enumerates the living</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>The other ten name him without requiring him.</strong> Fathers are named on their
      children&rsquo;s baptisms whether present or not. Grandfathers are named on grandchildren&rsquo;s.
      A marriage act names the bride&rsquo;s parents living or dead &mdash; his daughter Justa
      Pastora&rsquo;s of 1830 does <em>not</em> write him <em>difunto</em>, which is suggestive and is
      not evidence. He is named as an ancestor as late as <strong>1850</strong>, sixty-one years after
      that first christening.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>So 1812 is a floor and the trees&rsquo; 1832 is not a fact.</strong> This is
      the same test the whole register is now held to: <a href={u("/who/")}>being named in a later
      record is not evidence of being alive for it</a>, and of 293 people here only twenty are known to
      have died at all.</p>
  </div>
