<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-luis-carts">Luis Llerena, owner of carts and coaches</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The third Llerena in the census collection had an index entry giving <strong>a name, a sex, and
    nothing else</strong>. The page turned out not to be a census at all: it is a
    <strong>padrón of the owners of handcarts, wagons and coaches</strong> in a Montevideo district, and
    <strong>Luis Llerena is entry 10</strong>, with entry 11 marked <em>ídem</em> &mdash; which is how
    these lists repeat an owner for a second vehicle.</p>
    <p>That is worth knowing about the collection as much as about the man. <strong>A collection called
    <em>Census Records</em> holds property registers too, and the index does not say which is
    which</strong> &mdash; so an entry here is not evidence of residence, only of owning something.</p>
    <p><strong>The year is not secure and is not being guessed:</strong> the heading ends
    <em>&ldquo;de 18__&rdquo;</em>, and the last two digits read as 1841, 1842 or 1846. Nor is the man
    identified &mdash; this archive has two candidate Luises and the page offers no age, no household
    and no parents to choose between them.</p>
  </div>
