<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-alejandros">Four men called Alejandro &mdash; and a six-year gap that was never there</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The eldest known child of <strong>Avelino Lerena and Ambrosia Traibel</strong> was baptised
      <strong>Alejandro</strong> at La Mauricia on 7 January 1846. Somewhere in Argentina, on the
      enrolment cards of <a href={u("/argentina/")}>army drawer Caj&oacute;n 985</a>, a father is named
      <strong>Alejandro M. Lerena</strong>. This archive has asked since 14 September whether they are
      one man, and has always answered <em>inconclusive</em>.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>The answer stayed inconclusive for a reason that
      turned out to be false.</strong> The working log held the two apart on a <strong>six-year
      gap</strong> &mdash; a baptism pointing at 1843 or 1845, against a stated <em>&ldquo;born Uruguay
      <strong>1839</strong>&rdquo;</em>. That 1839 was graded <strong>PRIMARY (indexed)</strong> and
      sourced to the Capital Federal parish records. <strong>On 22 September all four of those records
      were opened, and every one of them leaves the father&rsquo;s age blank</strong> &mdash; while
      giving the mother&rsquo;s. The 1839 came from a <strong>family tree</strong>, and was re-graded as
      a record on the way in. It was also printed on this page, in the descent above, as a plain fact.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>Then he told us himself, twice.</strong> Two
      Montevideo birth acts of his own children were read off the plate &mdash; <strong>film 004596830,
      images 01806 and 02145</strong>. He stood in front of the officer and gave his age:
      <em>&ldquo;don Alejandro M. Lerena, de <strong>cuarenta y dos a&ntilde;os de edad</strong>, viudo,
      escribano, oriental, domiciliado en la calle San Jos&eacute; N 17&rdquo;</em> on 6 November 1886,
      and <em>&ldquo;don Alejandro M. Lerena <strong>de 43 a&ntilde;os de edad</strong> de estado viudo
      de nacionalidad Oriental&rdquo;</em> on 16 March 1888. Sixteen months apart, and consistent with
      each other. <strong>That puts his birth between 17 March and 6 November 1844.</strong></p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table style="min-width:78rem">
      <thead><tr>
        <th style="width:13rem">Who</th><th style="width:17rem">Born</th>
        <th style="width:9rem">Wife</th><th style="width:19rem">Children and records</th>
        <th style="width:20rem">His parents</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {alejandros.map((r) => { const c = Object.values(r); return (
          <tr>
            <th set:html={emph(c[0])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[1])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[2])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[3])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[4])} />
          </tr>); })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <strong>The gap is now one year, and it still cannot
      choose.</strong> Against a birth of 29 May 1843 he should have said 43 and 44; against 29 May 1845,
      41 and 42. He said <strong>42 and 43</strong>. The stated ages miss both candidate dates by exactly
      one year, <strong>in opposite directions</strong> &mdash; they sit precisely between them. A man
      consistently one year off a baptism act is the ordinary case and this archive sees it weekly. A man
      five years off, twice, is a different man or a bad number. <strong>So 1839 is gone, the mid-1840s
      are in, and that is the first thing that has ever pointed these two rows at each other.</strong>
      It is circumstantial, and they remain separate rows.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>The form asked this archive&rsquo;s exact
      question and nobody wrote the answer on it.</strong> The 1888 act is a printed sheet with a ruled
      line reading <em>&ldquo;Que es nieta por l&iacute;nea paterna de&nbsp;______&rdquo;</em>. It is
      <strong>blank</strong>, and so is the maternal one. That is common on these forms and is
      <strong>not</strong> evidence that he could not have answered.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>What the two acts did give is a route.</strong>
      He is <strong>viudo</strong> on both &mdash; a widower at forty-two, while Julia Bassani is
      twenty-two and <em>soltera</em>, living at a different address, where both children were born. So
      there is an earlier marriage and an earlier death in Montevideo, <strong>and a marriage act names
      the groom&rsquo;s parents</strong>. He is also an <strong>escribano</strong>, a notary, and
      notaries leave protocolos filed under their own names. Those are the two things left to try.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>And one row on this site was two men.</strong>
      The register carried a bare <em>&ldquo;Hector Lerena&rdquo;</em> of Buenos Aires who married
      <strong>Amalia Pacheco</strong>. His marriage act of 25 February 1911 at San Carlos was opened:
      <em>&ldquo;hijo leg&iacute;timo de D. <strong>Alejandro M&aacute;ximo</strong> &hellip; y de
      do&ntilde;a <strong>Julia Ba[ssani]</strong>, natural de Italia &hellip; con do&ntilde;a
      <strong>Amalia Pa[checo]</strong>&rdquo;</em>, the groom <em>&ldquo;de la Rep. Oriental, de veinte
      y cuatro&rdquo;</em>. The wife matches, the parents match, and twenty-four in February 1911 fits a
      birth on <strong>29 October 1886 at Montevideo</strong> exactly. <strong>They are one man, and the
      two rows are now one.</strong> The index had him born 1887 at Almagro, Buenos Aires; the act says
      <em>Oriental</em>, and the indexer had defaulted the birthplace to the place of the wedding.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>That act also spells out a letter.</strong> On
      the two Montevideo acts he is only ever <strong>Alejandro M. Lerena</strong>, and on the
      enrolment cards of Caj&oacute;n 985 he is only ever <strong>Alejandro M. Lerena</strong>. In the
      priest&rsquo;s hand at San Carlos in 1911 the initial is written out: <strong>M&aacute;ximo</strong>.
      <strong>So the <em>expansion</em> of that M. is now on a plate rather than in a tree, and the
      Alejandro M. Lerena of Caj&oacute;n 985 carries the same one.</strong> It is worth being exact about
      what that does and does not do: it joins the <em>cards</em> to the <em>Montevideo acts</em>, which
      were never in doubt &mdash; the same wife and the same children stand on both. It does not join
      either of them to Avelino&rsquo;s son.</p>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <strong>Which makes the family&rsquo;s road visible.</strong>
      H&eacute;ctor born Montevideo 1886, Emilia Alberta Montevideo 1888, Roberto Juan in
      <strong>Misiones</strong> 1891, R&oacute;mer Rosario in Buenos Aires 1895, then Elisa Cornelia
      1898, Carmen Romualda 1901 and Mar&iacute;a Luisa 1903 &mdash; the last three baptised together
      late, in 1905, 1907 and 1908. <strong>They left Montevideo between 1888 and 1891 and crossed the
      country before settling in the capital.</strong> Julia is <em>italiana</em> in 1886 and
      <em>austriaca</em> in 1888, aged 22 and then 24 &mdash; both written in front of her, and this
      archive records both rather than picking.</p>
  </div>
</section>

